  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/plicofollis_dussumieri.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Common name: Blacktip sea catfish</li><li>Description: Anal soft rays: 14–16. It has a prominent lateral ethmoid that forms a \"shelf\" between the eye and the nostrils, two pairs of vomerine toothplates, and a very short adipose-fin base.</li><li>Colouration: Body dark brown, lower surfaces completely covered with fine brown pigment specks.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1407",
      "html": "<p>Plicofollis dussumieri (Valenciennes in Cuvier &amp; Valenciennes, 1840). Type locality: Malabar, India. Size: 620 mm SL. IUCN Red List status: Least Concern.</p><p>Identification: It has a prominent lateral ethmoid that forms a \"shelf\" between the eye and the nostrils, two pairs of vomerine tooth plates, and a very short adipose-fin base. Body dark brown, lower surfaces completely covered with fine brown pigment specks.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/plicofollis_dussumieri.htm",
      "html": "<p>Indo-west Pacific: Mozambique and Madagascar to Sri Lanka, the east coast of India, Bangladesh, Myanmar to Sumatra.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1407",
      "html": "<p>Indian Ocean. Also found in lower reaches of rivers flowing into the ocean, from Mozambique and Madagascar to Sri Lanka, the east coast of India, Bangladesh, Myanmar to Sumatra.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/plicofollis_dussumieri.htm",
      "html": "<p>Found along the coast. Enters the lower parts of rivers of the east coast of Africa.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/plicofollis_dussumieri.htm",
      "html": "<p>Feeds on invertebrates and small fishes. Marketed fresh and dried-salted. Air bladder utilized for isinglass.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1407",
      "html": "<p>Feeds on invertebrates and small fishes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1407",
      "html": "<p>Unreported.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/plicofollis_dussumieri.htm",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: The genus name Plicofollis: plico, fold; follis, bellows or windbag, referring to creased or scalloped swim bladder characteristic of the genus. The specific name dussumieri: in honour of Jean-Jacques Dussumier (1792-1883), French voyager and merchant, who reported on this fish from off the Malabar coast of India.</p><p>IUCN Red List: This species is assessed as Least Concern due to its very large distribution and the lack of any known major widespread threats. Although it is utilised, there is no evidence at present that this poses a major threat to the species, however, further research into harvest levels would be beneficial in confirming this (IUCN 2017).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/plicofollis_dussumieri.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2008. FishBase. World Wide Web electronic publication. www.fishbase.org, version (12/2008).</li><li>Sparks, J.S., Kaymaram, F., Bishop, J., Al-Husaini, M., Almukhtar, M. &amp; Alam, S. 2017. Plicofollis dussumieri. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2017.</li><li>The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf.</li></ul>"
    },
